    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "29d78c7e_f5e2f3c8",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/MeshRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-14T16:32:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We could instead write an additional duplicate vertex here and for the first vertex to create two degenerate triangles. This would avoid flushing but it probably doesn\u0027t make a huge difference, since I imagine when we have support for graphite-backed meshes, they will live in unique buffers and be flushing anyways as part of binding new buffers.\n\nHowever, I realize now that the VerticesRenderStep doesn\u0027t deal with this problem because when it uses `VertState::Proc`, that iterates over the data respecting the primitive type and produces a triangle soup. So VerticesRenderStep actually has a bug in that we shouldn\u0027t let the RendererProvider choose based on the PrimitiveType, that is redundant and would actually produce this same problem if we drew things as a triangle soup interpreted as a triangle strip for instance.\n\nI believe we should do the same here, where (for now since it\u0027s only CPU -backed meshes) the MeshRenderStep itself will always have a triangle primitive type, and then its use of VertState::Proc handles the CPU-data\u0027s topology.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "c9d48ac10aa392fcf7c38e6f222885ec30f51d68",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
